     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily comes to 5,100 mg/day. That is double the usual maximum of 2,550 mg/day for immediate-release metformin, and a single 1,700 mg dose is also well above the normal maximum. Even with normal kidney and liver function, this raises a serious risk of lactic acidosis and severe GI toxicity.\n\n2. **The order needs a licensed prescriber.** You've said you aren't a clinician. Medication orders should come from, or be verified by, the treating prescriber.\n\nHere is what you can do:\n- If the dose is a typo, the prescriber can confirm the intended dose. Common regimens are 500 mg twice daily, 850 mg twice daily, or 1000 mg twice daily with meals, titrated as needed. The maximum is 2,550 mg/day.\n- If the prescriber does intend this dose, they should confirm it directly. Pharmacy should also review it before anything is entered.\n\nOnce I have a confirmed, safe dose from the prescriber, I can enter the order right away."
      }
     ],
